        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"gissing-the-odd-women-b59bd76c1c6b1593\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.95,\"analysis\":\"Bevis directly avows that he loves Monica, intensifying the asserted state with “with all my soul.” The declaration offers emotional reassurance but does not itself undertake the concrete commitment Monica seeks—leaving with her—and his accompanying proposal merely to discuss what can be done delays that decision. The exclamation and subsequent passion show intensity, not independent expressive/reflexive force.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The crisis setting gives the declaration some relational significance, but the text pointedly separates professed love from willingness to undertake the requested commitment.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Bevis directly addresses Monica in response to her challenge about whether he loves her enough to face the consequences of leaving together.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The avowal is elicited by Monica’s demand for love to be demonstrated through action. Bevis strongly professes love while shifting toward deliberation—“let us talk about it”—and subsequently emphasizes practical risks. Monica experiences this disparity between passionate avowal and concrete resolve as cowardice and disillusionment. His emotional and physical excitement may be genuine, but neither it nor the exclamatory delivery makes the words a reflex-like discharge, and the declaration does not itself promise elopement.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“I love you with all my soul, Monica!”\",\"supports\":\"A maximally explicit, intensified presentation of Bevis’s loving emotional state, supporting T=4.\",\"confidence\":1.0},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Monica immediately precedes the utterance with: “Don’t you love me enough to face whatever may happen?”\",\"supports\":\"The avowal is a responsive reassurance under challenge rather than an unprompted reflex-like verbal discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Bevis continues: “Sit down again, dearest; let us talk about it, and see what we can do.”\",\"supports\":\"He proposes discussion rather than undertaking the specific commitment to leave with Monica, weighing against P.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The narration says Monica finds “the unmanliness of his tone” a “dreadful disillusion” because she expected strength, courage, and eagerness to flee.\",\"supports\":\"The narrative explicitly contrasts Bevis’s declaration and passion with the practical commitment Monica expects.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"After the utterance he gives way to “amorous frenzy,” while later the temptation of the “basely selfish” natural man strengthens its hold on him.\",\"supports\":\"The surrounding passion and selfish temptation qualify Bevis’s motives and conduct but do not alter the core utterance’s dominant avowal force or independently establish E.\",\"confidence\":0.95}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T/P/E adequately represents the core utterance as a strong avowal without a corresponding undertaking or independently evidenced expressive/reflexive discharge. The mismatch between profession and action belongs to contextual interpretation rather than requiring O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
